<Base title="About Gwen" description="Meet Gwen, the creator of Critical Latitude. Published research, monitoring data, and clear reporting about the places people call home."><header class="page-heading topographic"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">THE PERSON BEHIND THE CHANNEL</p><h1>I'm Gwen.</h1><p>Science, explained through the places and people it affects.</p></div></header><section class="article-paper"><div class="article-width"><blockquote class="about-quote"><p>I'm Gwen. I make videos about earthquakes, volcanoes, tsunamis, glaciers and extreme weather, but most importantly, about the people whose lives sit on top of them.</p><p>Some of these are long, researched looks at one place and what the science says could happen there. Others are short updates when something is actually moving right now. Either way, I start with the published research and the monitoring data, and I explain it in plain English.</p><p>No predicted dates. No panic. Just what the instruments show, what scientists have concluded from it, and what is still unknown.</p><p>If there's a place or an event you want me to look at, tell me in the comments. I read them.</p><cite>Gwen · <a href={channelUrl}>Critical Latitude on YouTube</a></cite></blockquote><div class="prose about-method"><h2>How the reporting works</h2><p>The starting point is the published record. The articles distinguish measurements, scientific interpretation, planning scenarios, and questions that remain open. Each article links to its source video and the references available for that investigation.</p><p>The research and preparedness references include the <a href="https://www.usgs.gov/">USGS</a>, <a href="https://www.noaa.gov/">NOAA</a>, <a href="https://www.gfz.de/en/">GFZ</a>, <a href="https://www.fema.gov/">FEMA</a>, and the <a href="https://www.redcross.org/">American Red Cross</a>. Their inclusion identifies sources, not sponsorship or endorsement.</p><h2>Keep the uncertainty visible</h2><p>A scenario describes consequences under stated assumptions. A video is a dated investigation. Neither supplies a countdown or replaces current local warnings. Where the available record does not support a number, the article leaves that number out.</p><h2>Who makes this</h2><p>The most common question in the comments is whether I'm a real person. I am. My name is Gwen Silveira, the videos are produced by hand, and the person on screen is me.</p><p>The reporting is human work: reading the papers, checking the monitoring data, writing the script, and recording the narration. Production tools help with editing and graphics, the way they do for any small channel. What they never do is decide what a study says or invent a number.</p><p>If a claim on this site is wrong, the source is linked on every article. Check it, then tell me.</p><h2>Suggest a place</h2><p>Have a question about a coast, fault, mountain, or valley? Leave a comment on the <a href={channelUrl}>Critical Latitude channel</a>. That is where Gwen reads suggestions for future reporting.</p></div></div></section></Base>
